00:06:00,899 --> 00:06:12,899
When we represent inheritance in UMLN class diagrams, we use a solid body and a closed right triangle as the arrowhead.

52
00:06:12,899 --> 00:06:16,899
This inheritance relationship is always one way.

53
00:06:16,899 --> 00:06:19,755
It's always a one way relationship.

54
00:06:19,987 --> 00:06:31,768
We have the class A and we want to create a new class, in this case B, from that existing class A by using inheritance.

55
00:06:32,768 --> 00:06:41,708
The inheritance error begins from the new class and ends at the old class.

56
00:06:43,764 --> 00:06:52,884
The current terminology is the class A, we call it the parent class, and B is called child class.

57
00:06:53,764 --> 00:06:57,524
In Turkish we say ebeveyn sınıf ve çocuk sınıf.

58
00:06:58,803 --> 00:07:03,144
Or we can call the existing class a super class.

59
00:07:03,863 --> 00:07:09,803
And if we do so, then we refer to the new class as the subclass.

60
00:07:10,355 --> 00:07:14,855
Türkçe, we can say üst sınıf, alt sınıf.

61
00:07:15,435 --> 00:07:27,635
When we translate into Turkish, we sometimes use sınıf also, but most often we use üst class, üst sınıf, alt sınıf.

62
00:07:27,635 --> 00:07:38,355
And traditionally, when we draw inheritance relationship, we draw the parent class on top and derive new class.

63
00:07:38,355 --> 00:07:45,596
At the bottom, so we can call it top class, super class, and the sub class.

64
00:07:47,115 --> 00:07:51,195
And we can call the existing class as base class.

65
00:07:51,635 --> 00:07:55,255
Then we call the new class as derived class.

66
00:07:55,776 --> 00:07:59,455
In Turkish, we can say tamaslov ve tretilmiş.

67
00:08:00,675 --> 00:08:02,375
How does inheritance work?

68
00:08:02,720 --> 00:08:12,980
Every member field and every member method, in short, every member of the parent class are transferred to the child class.

69
00:08:13,840 --> 00:08:19,740
However, the children cannot access the inherited private members.

70
00:08:20,560 --> 00:08:28,540
If we want them to be able to do so, then we have to use public getter and all setter methods.

71
00:08:30,067 --> 00:08:37,748
And there is a special case of visibility rule. This is the protected rule.

72
00:08:38,748 --> 00:08:55,707
If a member network is declared as protected or at the same time member field or a member network is declared as protected, then those members act.

73
00:08:56,063 --> 00:09:03,563
Like public numbers to the child classes and private to other classes.

74
00:09:07,827 --> 00:09:11,827
What are the rules? There are some important rules for inheritance.

75
00:09:13,487 --> 00:09:18,107
A subclass cannot reject a number from the superclass.

76
00:09:19,387 --> 00:09:29,827
This is why we translate this relationship as kalıtım to Turkish, in similar to its biological fashion.

77
00:09:30,740 --> 00:09:39,399
If we think about it is legal fashion, you can reject some inheritance.

78
00:09:39,399 --> 00:09:50,319
For example, your parents have left some debt behind and you can reject that.

79
00:09:51,635 --> 00:09:58,875
But as for the genetic properties, you cannot reject anything.

80
00:10:01,115 --> 00:10:09,295
But for member methods, you can change the body of an inherited method.

81
00:10:10,195 --> 00:10:15,035
And this process is called overriding, with one exception.

82
00:10:15,796 --> 00:10:24,436
If you declare a member method as final, then this method cannot be overridden in child class.

83
00:10:25,995 --> 00:10:28,896
Therefore, we have covered the last exceptions.

84
00:10:28,896 --> 00:10:31,596
What does protected means?

85
00:10:31,995 --> 00:10:34,216
It is related with inheritance.

86
00:10:35,036 --> 00:10:41,896
Protected members mean public to subclasses and private to others.

87
00:10:42,548 --> 00:10:51,107
And final member method means you cannot change the body of that method in a subclass.

88
00:10:53,788 --> 00:10:56,168
Let me remind you that.

89
00:10:56,768 --> 00:11:03,508
And let me give you an example about how you cannot reject inheritance.

90
00:11:05,491 --> 00:11:10,571
For example, I have inherited my eye color from my father.

91
00:11:10,571 --> 00:11:19,411
It's light brown, but my mother had green eyes.

92
00:11:19,827 --> 00:11:36,548
Maybe I wish to have green eyes too, but I cannot reject my inheritance. I cannot reject my eyes. I have obtained their genetic material from my father. I cannot reject it. There is no way.

93
00:11:37,076 --> 00:11:47,796
But, if I wish, I can take a colored lens, a green one or a blue one, and use it and have my eye color changed externally.

94
00:11:49,236 --> 00:11:52,096
And this is an example of override.

95
00:11:52,096 --> 00:11:53,096
Okay.

96
00:11:53,652 --> 00:12:05,552
This is why we use the genetic translation such as kalitim, Turkish, not its legal version, miras.

97
00:12:10,752 --> 00:12:12,752
This is one rule.

98
00:12:13,232 --> 00:12:18,552
And the second rule is, of course, you can add new members to subclasses.

99
00:12:19,923 --> 00:12:28,524
And you can use a subclass as a parent of other child classes as well.

100
00:12:29,123 --> 00:12:35,563
In that case, we come up with a hierarchy or a tree you can call.

101
00:12:36,123 --> 00:12:40,463
For example, in my slide, we have the class person.

102
00:12:41,395 --> 00:12:52,775
And by using this class person, we have created three new classes by inheritance, student, employer and retired.

103
00:12:53,971 --> 00:13:09,532
And by using the employee class, we have created two new additional subclasses of those employee class, which are directors and part-time.

104
00:13:10,652 --> 00:13:16,812
In that case, the class employee is the subclass of class person.

105
00:13:17,268 --> 00:13:22,788
And it is the superclass of classes, director and part-time classes.

106
00:13:24,107 --> 00:13:33,487
We call this as a hierarchy or a tree, as you can remember from the data structure class.

107
00:13:34,087 --> 00:13:42,947
But if you didn't learn the tree structure yet, then you will be learning it soon in data structure class.

108
00:13:46,196 --> 00:13:54,116
When using inheritance, please do not let this inheritance hierarchy too deep.

109
00:13:54,116 --> 00:14:02,036
Because it creates a problem which is called fragile superclass problem.

110
00:14:02,516 --> 00:14:06,836
Why? This is analogous to a rotten tree.

111
00:14:07,604 --> 00:14:14,084
In data structures, we call the topmost node as the root.

112
00:14:16,624 --> 00:14:32,924
In regular trees, biological trees, the roots are at the ground, but in data structure trees or hierarchies, the root is the utmost numbers, the utmost element, the element on the top.

113
00:14:33,299 --> 00:14:38,839
In that case, this person class is our root of the hierarchy.

114
00:14:39,839 --> 00:14:49,239
Do not make this hierarchy too deep because as every class inherits all the properties of the parent class,

115
00:14:49,940 --> 00:15:17,940
When this inheritance becomes too many levels, then when you make some change in the root class or utmost classes, then those changes can create some unintended consequences in other methods, in other subclasses, because those subclasses...

116
00:15:17,940 --> 00:15:21,060
Classes can have additional methods as well.

117
00:15:21,060 --> 00:15:27,980
And those additional methods, some of them will be using the inherited methods.

118
00:15:27,980 --> 00:15:37,860
And when you change something in the root, then this change will be propagated during the entire hierarchy.

119
00:15:37,860 --> 00:15:45,780
And somewhere in between, previously correctly working member methods can...

120
00:16:18,548 --> 00:16:25,827
We can call the inheritance relationship as the generalization specialization relationship as well.

121
00:16:26,488 --> 00:16:33,707
Why? Because a child class is a specialized and more capable version of its parents.

122
00:16:34,368 --> 00:16:45,827
Because a child class has every member from the parent class and in addition it can have additional member fields and methods.

123
00:16:46,192 --> 00:16:54,231
Therefore, that child is more specialized, it has more capabilities than its parents.

124
00:16:55,292 --> 00:17:06,392
And likewise, when you look from the other side, a parent class is a generalized version of its children having less members.

125
00:17:09,971 --> 00:17:25,652
And another effect of this relationship is substitutability, which means a child can be used instead of its parent wherever a parent is expected.

126
00:17:26,067 --> 00:17:31,347
Why? Because that child has everything that the parent has.

127
00:17:31,347 --> 00:17:37,907
So it can be used, it can do whatever its parent can do.

128
00:17:38,487 --> 00:17:44,907
So it can be used in any context that an object from a parent class is being expected.

129
00:17:46,087 --> 00:17:51,288
And therefore, we can give another name to that relationship, is-air relationship.

130
00:17:52,127 --> 00:17:52,987
For example...

131
00:17:54,804 --> 00:18:04,504
We have the car class and we have drive three subclasses from it, station wagon, racing and jeeps.

132
00:18:05,844 --> 00:18:07,504
Let's consider the railroad.

133
00:18:10,804 --> 00:18:15,804
For example, a race car is a specialized version of a regular car.

134
00:18:16,604 --> 00:18:21,604
It has more speed, maybe it has more gears.

135
00:18:22,067 --> 00:18:47,907
It is only suitable for one driver, doesn't have any baggages. Its fuel depot may be smaller than a regular car, but according to the race type, it can have different wheels, wheels having different widths, for example.

136
00:18:48,788 --> 00:18:50,028
Exeter.

137
00:18:51,168 --> 00:18:56,108
Therefore, you can say a race car is a specialized version of a regular car.

138
00:18:58,208 --> 00:19:02,488
Or we can say every race car is a car.

139
00:19:03,048 --> 00:19:05,768
Every race car is also a car.

140
00:19:06,168 --> 00:19:12,128
Therefore, you can name this relationship as is a relationship too.

141
00:19:13,508 --> 00:19:16,268
Or every jeep is a car.

142
00:19:16,692 --> 00:19:23,152
It is most suited to traverse through rough terrain, but it's a car.

143
00:19:23,392 --> 00:19:25,172
It takes four or five passengers.

144
00:19:25,412 --> 00:19:26,832
You can carry luggage, etc.

145
00:19:27,092 --> 00:19:32,711
But if your luggage is too much, then it's better for you to use a station wagon car.

146
00:19:33,731 --> 00:19:37,192
That station wagon, every station wagon car is a car.

147
00:19:37,332 --> 00:19:38,292
It's also a car.

148
00:19:38,771 --> 00:19:52,311
And a station wagon car is a more specialized version of a car which has more interior space, more trunk space, and in sometimes more passenger seats.

149
00:19:54,491 --> 00:20:01,991
You want to go from point A to point B. If you need to carry a lot of load, you use a station wagon.

150
00:20:02,548 --> 00:20:07,007
If you want to go there as soon as possible, you can use a race car.

151
00:20:07,647 --> 00:20:20,147
Or if your destination is in some rough spaces, for example, mountain terrain or sand terrain, for example, then you should better use it.

152
00:20:20,947 --> 00:20:28,827
But you can use every instance of those subclasses as a car too.

153
